WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.840
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.100
A census taker visits
the home of a mathematician

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.320
and asks for the ages
of his children.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.680
The mathematician offers
up a riddle:

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.440
"If you add up the ages
of my 3 children,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.700
you get my house number,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.020
and if you multiply the ages,
you get the result 72."

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.520
The census taker thinks about it

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.720
and replies he still doesn't know
the ages of the children.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.480
The mathematician says,
"Of course not."

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.520
"I forgot to mention my oldest child
loves chocolate chip cookies."

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.740
Suddenly the census taker says
he gets it,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.100
and he writes down the 3 ages
of the children.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.060
What are the ages of the children?

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.820
Give this puzzle a try,
and when you're ready,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.740
keep watching the video
for the solution.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.560
Like any good puzzle,
you have to deduce

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.340
some of the details
from the story.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.980
First you have to deduce
that the census taker

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.260
knows the house number
of the mathematician.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.880
This is a census taker
who should know the address

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.620
and he's also visiting the mathematician
at his home.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.000
The other detail,
which is a little more controversial,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.720
but is generally common sense,

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.540
is that you have to conclude the mathematician has an oldest child with a distinct age,

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.080
so not twins,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.760
and not two children
that are born almost a year apart.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.420
This comes from the detail where the mathematician says his oldest child

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.100
loves chocolate chip cookies.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.200
This implies the mathematician does not have two oldest children who are twins,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.280
because you wouldn't generally
refer to twins as

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.680
being the oldest child,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.920
even though one of the children

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.080
is probably older by a few minutes.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.400
And you could also have a child
who's born, say,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.900
9 to 12 months apart,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.600
and they could possibly be
the same age,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.080
even though they're not exactly
born in the same...

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.920
you know, they're not twins.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.220
But commonly,
this doesn't happen.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.420
So in this story we're going to say
the mathematician has an oldest child

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.580
with a distinct age,

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.520
and that's gonna give the census taker

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.800
the detail he needs.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.400
So how can the census taker
figure this out?

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.180
The mathematician says the three children

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.120
have a product of their ages of 72.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.680
We can factor 72 into the primes

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.420
2 times 2 times 2
times 3 times 3.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.280
There are only a few prime factors

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.300
and so when we consider the way that you can get 3 different ages to multiply to be 72,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.980
there are actually only 12 different possibilities.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.140
These are written
in increasing order.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.280
You could have 1, 1, 72,
1, 2, 36,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.800
1, 3, 24,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.940
1, 4, 18,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.080
1, 6, 12,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.020
1, 8, 9,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.080
2, 2, 18,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.760
2, 3, 12,
2, 4, 9,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.840
2, 6, 6,
3, 3, 8,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.080
or 3, 4, 6.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.280
The census taker could work out the sum of the three ages

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.860
to get these results.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.180
So now,

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.980
here's what goes through the census taker's mind.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.400
The mathematician says if you add up the three ages,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.260
you get the house number.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.700
So if the house number was
anything besides 14,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.320
the census taker would uniquely be able to identify

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.160
the three ages of the children.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.080
If the house number was,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.200
uh, 22 for example,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.000
the census taker would know

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.560
the children have to be age 2, 2, and 18.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.180
Or if the house number was 15,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.760
the census taker would know

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.880
the ages would have to be
2, 4, 9,

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.680
because there's no other way you could get to that sum.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.720
So when the census taker says

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.780
he's tried to work it out, but he still can't figure it out,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.240
knowing the house number,

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.800
that means the house number has to be 14.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.220
So the census taker is trying to figure out

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.620
whether the 3 kids are age
2, 6, and 6,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.140
or 3, 3, and 8.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.500
And this is where the detail that
the oldest child

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.580
loves chocolate chip cookies
comes into play.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.820
The "oldest child" implies there's one child who has a distinct age

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.520
that's the oldest child.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.180
So we can rule out the possibility of 2, 6, and 6,

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.580
and that means the answer
is 3, 3, and 8.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.340
Did you figure it out?

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.580
